{
  "slug": "design-circular-queue",
  "title": "Design Circular Queue",
  "topic": "data-structures",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "design / fixed-capacity fifo simulation with flat command stream",
  "statement": "A queue of fixed `capacity` slots stores integers in first-in first-out order and refuses a new item when it already holds `capacity` items. You are given the capacity and a list of commands `ops`; each command yields exactly one integer result, and the results are returned in command order.\n\n- `ops[i] > 0` - enqueue the value `ops[i]`. Result `1` if it was stored, `0` if the queue was full.\n- `ops[i] == 0` - dequeue and return the front value, or `-1` if the queue is empty.\n- `ops[i] == -1` - return the current front value without removing it, or `-1` if empty.\n- `ops[i] == -2` - return the most recently stored value without removing it, or `-1` if empty.\n- `ops[i] == -3` - return `1` if the queue is empty, otherwise `0`.\n- `ops[i] == -4` - return `1` if the queue currently holds `capacity` items, otherwise `0`.\n- `ops[i] == -5` - return how many items the queue currently holds.\n\nReturn the array of results, with one entry per command.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= capacity <= 100`, `1 <= ops.length <= 200` and `-5 <= ops[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "capacity = 1, ops = [-5]",
      "output": "[0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "capacity = 29, ops = [1158]",
      "output": "[1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(capacity: number, ops: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Separate the state into three pieces: the array of slots, an index pointing at the front, and a count of live items. Derive the back index from those instead of storing it.",
    "Because the array is fixed at `capacity`, advancing any index must wrap with a modulo so a slot freed at the front is reused at the back.",
    "Empty and full are distinguishable only through the count. Test the count before computing a rear index, since a stale value sits at (head + size - 1) % capacity when the queue is empty."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force keeps the queue in a growable list: enqueue appends to the back, dequeue removes from the front, and front/rear/size/isEmpty/isFull are direct reads. Removing from the front of a list moves every remaining element down one slot, so each dequeue costs O(n) and the whole run costs O(ops.length * capacity). The optimal is a ring buffer: a fixed array of exactly `capacity` slots, an index `head` for the front, and a counter `size`. The slot after the current back is (head + size) % capacity, so enqueue writes there and bumps size, dequeue reads ring[head], advances head modulo capacity, and decrements size, and the rear sits at (head + size - 1) % capacity. No element ever moves.",
    "why_optimal": "Enqueue, dequeue, front, rear, size, isEmpty and isFull are all constant-time reads or writes of a slot and two counters, so O(ops.length) total is the floor for a simulation that must emit one result per command. The brute force is not wrong, only wasteful: the shift on every dequeue is the entire extra factor, which grows with capacity up to 100.",
    "pitfalls": "Writing the back slot as (head + size) without the modulo overflows the array as soon as the buffer wraps, and computing the rear as head + size - 1 without modulo points past the end after a wrap. When size is 0 the rear index (head + size - 1) % capacity is still a valid slot holding stale data, so the empty check has to come before the read, not after. capacity can be 1, so the full and empty checks must both work on the same single slot. A successful enqueue reports `1` rather than the value it stored, and a rejected enqueue still emits `0` instead of being skipped."
  },
